CAT = "caautoNetwork"; SEG = "NET"
# RSOS 2023 MWA C: Diagnoses and repairs vehicle module communications systems (C-10, C-11)
Q = [
("With the battery disconnected, what resistance is normal between DLC pins 6 and 14 on a high-speed CAN bus?",
 "About 60 ohms", ["About 120 ohms", "About 240 ohms", "Under 1 ohm"],
 "Two 120-ohm terminating resistors sit in parallel across CAN high and CAN low, giving about 60 ohms. A reading of 120 ohms means one resistor or its branch is open."),
("A high-speed CAN bus measures 120 ohms between CAN high and CAN low. What does this suggest?",
 "One end resistor is open", ["The bus reading is normal", "CAN high is shorted to ground", "CAN high and low are shorted"],
 "Only one resistor is being measured, so the other end of the bus or its resistor is open. A short between the two lines would read near zero."),
("Which DLC pin supplies battery power to a scan tool?",
 "Pin 16", ["Pin 4", "Pin 6", "Pin 14"],
 "The OBD-II connector has unswitched battery positive at pin 16. Pins 4 and 5 are grounds, and 6 and 14 are CAN high and CAN low."),
("At rest, about what voltage do both CAN high and CAN low sit at on a high-speed bus?",
 "About 2.5 V", ["About 0 V", "About 5 V", "About 12 V"],
 "Both lines idle near 2.5 V. When a dominant bit is sent, CAN high rises toward 3.5 V and CAN low falls toward 1.5 V."),
("Why are the CAN high and CAN low wires twisted together?",
 "To cancel electrical noise", ["To make them easier to route", "To raise the bus voltage", "To reduce wire weight"],
 "Twisting exposes both wires to the same interference, and because the receivers read the difference between them, the noise cancels out."),
("What is the main job of a gateway module?",
 "To pass data between networks", ["To store fault codes only", "To supply 5 V references", "To control the starter relay"],
 "Vehicles run several networks at different speeds. The gateway translates and forwards messages so modules on different buses can share data."),
("How does a LIN bus mainly differ from CAN?",
 "One wire and a master node", ["Two twisted wire pairs", "A fibre optic cable loop", "No ground circuit at all"],
 "LIN is a low-cost single-wire bus where one master module polls slave devices such as window or mirror motors. It is far slower than CAN."),
("What type of fault codes start with the letter U?",
 "Network", ["Powertrain", "Body", "Chassis"],
 "U codes cover network and module communication faults, such as lost communication with a module. P, B and C are powertrain, body and chassis."),
("Before reprogramming a module, what is the most important step for the vehicle's battery?",
 "Connect a stable power supply", ["Disconnect the negative cable", "Run the engine at idle", "Turn on the headlights"],
 "A voltage drop during a flash can corrupt the software and leave the module unusable. A programming power supply holds voltage steady for the whole session."),
("What is a J2534 pass-thru device used for?",
 "Flashing module software", ["Measuring parasitic draw", "Testing ignition coils", "Charging the 12 V battery"],
 "J2534 is a standard interface that lets a laptop running the maker's software reprogram and communicate with vehicle modules."),
("A scan tool will not communicate with any module. What should you check first?",
 "Power and ground at the DLC", ["The module software level", "The fuel pump relay", "The wheel speed sensors"],
 "Without power at pin 16 and good grounds at pins 4 and 5, the tool cannot work at all. A blown fuse feeding the DLC is a common cause."),
("Which tool shows whether CAN messages are actually toggling on the bus?",
 "A lab scope", ["A test light", "An ohmmeter", "A fused jumper"],
 "A digital storage oscilloscope shows both CAN lines and their mirror-image switching in real time. A test light can load the bus and cannot show the data."),
("Why should a test light never be used on a data bus line?",
 "It loads down the bus", ["It cannot light on 12 V", "It reads only AC voltage", "It needs a separate battery"],
 "An incandescent test light draws current and pulls the line voltage down, which can corrupt messages and damage module transceivers. Use a high-impedance meter or scope."),
("What is often the first step after installing a new module?",
 "Programming the module", ["Clearing all fuel trims", "Bleeding the brakes", "Replacing the gateway"],
 "Many replacement modules arrive blank and need software, vehicle configuration or the VIN written to them before they will work on the network."),
("A replacement body module is installed but the vehicle will not start. What is a likely cause?",
 "No immobilizer relearn", ["The CAN resistors are reversed", "The fuel trims are too high", "The battery is overcharged"],
 "The immobilizer checks that keys and modules recognize each other. A new module must be paired through a security relearn before the engine will run."),
("Where are the CAN terminating resistors usually located?",
 "In the two end modules", ["In the fuse box only", "Inside the DLC connector", "In the scan tool cable"],
 "Most designs put a 120-ohm resistor inside the two modules at the physical ends of the bus. Some vehicles use a separate resistor in the harness instead."),
("A network fault comes and goes with a door opening. What is a likely cause?",
 "A chafed door harness", ["A failed engine module", "A low fuel tank level", "A weak ignition coil"],
 "Wires flex every time a door opens and can break or short where they pass into the body. An intermittent fault tied to that movement points to the harness boot."),
("What is a typical high-speed CAN bus data rate in a modern vehicle?",
 "500 kilobits per second", ["10 kilobits per second", "50 megabits per second", "9600 bits per second"],
 "Powertrain and chassis high-speed CAN commonly run at 500 kbit/s. LIN runs at up to about 20 kbit/s."),
("On a module that will not wake, what should you check before condemning it?",
 "Its power and ground feeds", ["Its software version date", "Its part number on the label", "Its mounting bolt torque"],
 "A module needs battery power, ignition or wake-up signal and good grounds to talk on the bus. A missing feed is far more common than a failed module."),
("A data bus wire is repaired. What is the recommended practice?",
 "Keep the twist, seal the splice", ["Untwist the wires for access", "Use a larger gauge of wire", "Add a resistor at the splice"],
 "Makers require the original twist rate to be kept close to the repair and the splice to be sealed against moisture. Long untwisted runs let noise into the bus."),
]
